.Dt OSSL-GUIDE-LIBSSL-INTRODUCTION 7ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ossl-guide-libssl-introduction ,
.Nm ssl
.Nd OpenSSL Guide: \&An introduction to libssl
.Sh INTRODUCTION
The OpenSSL
.Qo Li libssl Qc
library provides implementations of several secure network
communications protocols.
Specifically it provides SSL/TLS (SSLv3, TLSv1, TLSv1.1, TLSv1.2 and
TLSv1.3), DTLS (DTLSv1 and DTLSv1.2) and QUIC (client side only).
The library depends on
.Qo Li libcrypto Qc
for its underlying cryptographic operations (see
.Xr ossl-guide-libcrypto-introduction 7 Ns ).
.Pp
The set of APIs supplied by
.Qo Li libssl Qc
is common across all of these different network protocols, so a
developer familiar with writing applications using one of these
protocols should be able to transition to using another with relative
ease.
.Pp
An application written to use
.Qo Li libssl Qc
will include the
.Pa <openssl/ssl.h>
header file and will typically use two main data structures, i.e.
.Sy SSL
and
.Sy SSL_CTX .
.Pp
An
.Sy SSL
object is used to represent a connection to a remote peer.
Once a connection with a remote peer has been established data can be
exchanged with that peer.
.Pp
When using DTLS any data that is exchanged uses "datagram" semantics,
i.e.
the packets of data can be delivered in any order, and they are not
guaranteed to arrive at all.
In this case the
.Sy SSL
object used for the connection is also used for exchanging data with the
peer.
.Pp
Both TLS and QUIC support the concept of a "stream" of data.
Data sent via a stream is guaranteed to be delivered in order without
any data loss.
A stream can be uni- or bi-directional.
.Pp
SSL/TLS only supports one stream of data per connection and it is always
bi-directional.
In this case the
.Sy SSL
object used for the connection also represents that stream.
See
.Xr ossl-guide-tls-introduction 7
for more information.
.Pp
The QUIC protocol can support multiple streams per connection and they
can be uni- or bi-directional.
In this case an
.Sy SSL
object can represent the underlying connection, or a stream, or both.
Where multiple streams are in use a separate
.Sy SSL
object is used for each one.
See
.Xr ossl-guide-quic-introduction 7
for more information.
.Pp
An
.Sy SSL_CTX
object is used to create the
.Sy SSL
object for the underlying connection.
A single
.Sy SSL_CTX
object can be used to create many connections (each represented by a
separate
.Sy SSL
object).
Many API functions in libssl exist in two forms: one that takes an
.Sy SSL_CTX
and one that takes an
.Sy SSL .
Typically settings that you apply to the
.Sy SSL_CTX
will then be inherited by any
.Sy SSL
object that you create from it.
Alternatively you can apply settings directly to the
.Sy SSL
object without affecting other
.Sy SSL
objects.
Note that you should not normally make changes to an
.Sy SSL_CTX
after the first
.Sy SSL
object has been created from it.
.Sh DATA STRUCTURES
As well as
.Sy SSL_CTX
and
.Sy SSL
there are a number of other data structures that an application may need
to use.
They are summarised below.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy SSL_METHOD No (SSL Method)
This structure is used to indicate the kind of connection you want to
make, e.g.
whether it is to represent the client or the server, and whether it is
to use SSL/TLS, DTLS or QUIC. It is passed as a parameter when creating
the
.Sy SSL_CTX .
.It Sy SSL_SESSION No (SSL Session)
After establishing a connection with a peer the agreed cryptographic
material can be reused to create future connections with the same peer
more rapidly.
The set of data used for such a future connection establishment attempt
is collected together into an
.Sy SSL_SESSION
object.
A single successful connection with a peer may generate zero or more
such
.Sy SSL_SESSION
objects for use in future connection attempts.
.It Sy SSL_CIPHER No (SSL Cipher)
During connection establishment the client and server agree upon
cryptographic algorithms they are going to use for encryption and other
uses.
A single set of cryptographic algorithms that are to be used together is
known as a ciphersuite.
Such a set is represented by an
.Sy SSL_CIPHER
object.
.Pp
The set of available ciphersuites that can be used are configured in the
.Sy SSL_CTX
or
.Sy SSL .
.El
